\begin_layout Title
General Equilibrium: Homework 2 (2012)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Problem
Consider an economy with two goods 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 and an arbitrary number of agents.
 Assume preferences are stricly increasing in each good.
 Consider two cases:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Problem
(a) 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is perfectly divisible but 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 comes only in integer units.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Problem
(b) Both 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 come only in integer units.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Problem
(Answer separetly for case (a) and case (b)).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Problem
Could we have a competitive equilibrium allocation that is not Pareto optimal?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Problem
If your answer is 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

yes,
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 give an example with a competitive equilibrium allocation that is not Pareto
 optimal.
 (Justify both facts: (i) that the allocation in your example is a competitive
 equilibrium allocation, and (ii) that it is not Pareto optimal.)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part a.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Suppose 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is perfectly divisible, but 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 comes only in integer units.
 In this case, local non-satiation still holds for each agent
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Take an agent 
\begin_inset Formula $i\in N$
\end_inset

 and assume 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim_{i}$
\end_inset

 is strictly increasing in each good.
 Take 
\begin_inset Formula $(x_{i},y_{i})\in\mathbb{R}_{+}\times\mathbb{Z}_{+}$
\end_inset

 and take 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon\in\mathbb{R}_{++}$
\end_inset

.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $(\tilde{x}_{i},y_{i})\equiv(x_{i}+\nicefrac{\epsilon}{2},y_{i})$
\end_inset

.
 Note, 
\begin_inset Formula $(\tilde{x}_{i},y_{i})\in B_{\epsilon}((x_{i},y_{i}))$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $(\tilde{x}_{i},y_{i})\gneq(x_{i},y_{i})$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim_{i}$
\end_inset

 is strictly increasing in each good, then 
\begin_inset Formula $(\tilde{x}_{i},y_{i})\gneq(x_{i},y_{i})\implies(\tilde{x}_{i},y_{i})\succ_{i}(x_{i},y_{i})$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim_{i}$
\end_inset

 is locally non-satiated.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Since local non-satiation still holds, then by the First Fundamental Theorem
 of Welfare Economics, if 
\begin_inset Formula $(x,p)$
\end_inset

 is a competitive equilibrium, then it is Pareto efficient.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Assume 
\begin_inset Formula $(x,y,p)$
\end_inset

 is a competitive equilibrium.
 Suppose 
\begin_inset Formula $(x,y)$
\end_inset

 is not Pareto optimal.
 Then, there exists a feasible allocation 
\begin_inset Formula $(x^{\prime},y^{\prime})$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ i:(x_{i}^{\prime},y_{i}^{\prime})\succsim(x_{i},y_{i})$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\exists\ k:(x_{k}^{\prime},y_{k}^{\prime})\succ_{k}(x_{k},y_{k})$
\end_inset

.
 By local non-satiation, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
(p_{x},p_{y})\cdot(x_{i}^{\prime},y_{i}^{\prime})\geq(p_{x},p_{y})\cdot(w_{x},w_{y})\ \forall\ i
\]

\end_inset

and by 
\begin_inset Formula $(x_{k}^{\prime},y_{k}^{\prime})\succ_{k}(x_{k},y_{k})$
\end_inset

, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
(p_{x},p_{y})\cdot(x_{k}^{\prime},y_{k}^{\prime})>(p_{x},p_{y})\cdot(w_{x},w_{y})
\]

\end_inset

Thus,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}(p_{x},p_{y})\cdot(x_{i}^{\prime},y_{i}^{\prime})>{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}(p_{x},p_{y})\cdot(w_{x},w_{y})
\]

\end_inset

This contradicts the fact that 
\begin_inset Formula $(x^{\prime},y^{\prime})$
\end_inset

 is feasible.
 Therefore, 
\begin_inset Formula $(x,y)$
\end_inset

 is Pareto optimal.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part b.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Next, suppose that both 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 can only be consumed in integer units.
 In this case, there may be a competitive equilibrium that is not Pareto
 optimal.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For example, suppose that:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\omega_{x1}=\omega_{x2}=\omega_{y1}=\omega_{y2}=1$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $p_{x}=2$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{y}=1$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $u_{1}(x_{1},y_{1})=x_{1}+y_{1}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $u_{2}(x_{1},y_{1})=10x_{1}+y_{1}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Note that:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $(1,1)\in\begin{array}[t]{rl}
\textrm{argmax} & u_{1}(x_{1},y_{1})\\
\textrm{s.t.} & 2x_{1}+y_{1}\leq3
\end{array}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $(1,1)\in\begin{array}[t]{rl}
\textrm{argmax} & u_{2}(x_{2},y_{2})\\
\textrm{s.t.} & 2x_{1}+y_{1}\leq3
\end{array}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $(1,1)+(1,1)=(2,2)=(\hat{\omega}_{x},\hat{\omega}_{y})$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
So, we have a competitive equilibrium at 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1}=x_{2}=y_{1}=y_{2}=1$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
However, we also have:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $u_{1}(0,2)=u_{1}(1,1)$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $u_{2}(2,0)>u_{2}(1,1)$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $(2,0)+(0,2)=(2,2)=(\hat{\omega}_{x},\hat{\omega}_{y})$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Thus, the aforementioned competitive equilibrium is not Pareto optimal.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Problem
Consider a two-person, two-commodity economy with
\end_layout

\begin_layout Problem
\begin_inset Formula $X_{i}=\left\{ x_{i}\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{2}:x_{i1}+x_{i2}\geq1\right\} ,\ i=1,2$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Problem
\begin_inset Formula $w_{1}=(1,0)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{2}=(1,2)$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Problem
\begin_inset Formula $u_{1}=x_{11}+2x_{12}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{2}=\ln x_{21}+\ln x_{22}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

