\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $p\equiv\frac{p_{2}}{p_{1}}$
\end_inset

.
 Since each agent's preferences are monotone, then they are locally non-satiatie
d.
 So, the budget constraint holds with equality for both consumers, and thus,
 Walras's Law holds.
 To calculate the demand function of an agent, we maximize utility function
 subject to his budget constraint.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The maximization problem for agent 1 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\max & u_{1}=x_{11}+2x_{12}\\
\mbox{s.t.} & x_{11}+px_{12}=1
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Since the agent's utility funciton is linear, then, taking into account
 the binding constraint on the consumption set, his demand function is given
 by
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
x_{1}(p)=\begin{cases}
\left(\begin{array}{c}
0\\
\frac{1}{p}
\end{array}\right) & \mbox{if}\ p<2\\
\left(\begin{array}{c}
x_{11}\\
\frac{1}{2}-\frac{x_{11}}{2}
\end{array}\right),\ x_{11}\in\left[0,1\right] & \mbox{if}\ p=2\\
\left(\begin{array}{c}
1\\
0
\end{array}\right) & \mbox{if}\ p>2
\end{cases}
\]

\end_inset

For agent 2, any monotonic transformation of 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{2}$
\end_inset

 still represents his preferences.
 So, we will consider the function 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(e^{\frac{1}{2}u_{2}(\cdot)}\right)$
\end_inset

.
 So, the maximization problem for agent 2 is 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\max & u_{2}=x_{21}^{\nicefrac{1}{2}}x_{22}^{\nicefrac{1}{2}}\\
\mbox{s.t.} & x_{21}+px_{22}=1+2p
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Since the agent's utility funciton is Cobb-Douglas, then his demand function
 is given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
x_{2}(p)=\left(\begin{array}{c}
\frac{1}{2}+p\\
\frac{1}{2p}+1
\end{array}\right)
\]

\end_inset

Note, in this situation, the constraint on the consumption set is not binding.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part c.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Since 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}(x_{i})$
\end_inset

 is differentiable and quasi-concave, then a necessary and sufficient condition
 for the feasible interior points to be Parteo optimal (PO) is that 
\begin_inset Formula $MRS_{x_{11,}x_{12}}=MRS_{x_{21},x_{22}}$
\end_inset

.
 Note, 
\begin_inset Formula $MRS_{x_{11},x_{12}}=\frac{1}{2}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $MRS_{x_{21},x_{22}}=\frac{x_{22}}{x_{21}}$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, the set of interior Pareto optimal points is given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
P0=\left\{ (x_{1},x_{2})\in X_{1}\times X_{2}:x_{1}+x_{2}=(2,2)^{\prime}\ \wedge\ 2x_{22}=x_{21}\right\} 
\]

\end_inset

To find the boundary Pareto optima, we concentrate on the area where each
 agent has the highest utility.
 We can also find the extreme Pareto optima by solving the Lagrangian with
 proper constraints.
 
\end_layout

