\begin_layout Standard
A good way to think about the boundary solutions for the Pareto optima is
 that the boundary is tangential to the indifference curves of agent 2 and/or
 the boundary intersects the indifference curves of agent 1.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part d.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To find the competitive equilibrium, we must find all 
\begin_inset Formula $p\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{2}$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{z}(p)=\hat{x}(p)-\hat{w}=0$
\end_inset

.
 By Walras' Law, it is enough to show only one markets clearing for 
\begin_inset Formula $L=2$
\end_inset

.
 Let us consider the two cases for 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\emph on
\bar under
Case:
\emph default
\bar default
 
\begin_inset Formula $p<2$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1}(p)=\left(\begin{array}{cc}
0 & \frac{1}{p}\end{array}\right)^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{2}(p)=\left(\begin{array}{cc}
\frac{1}{2}+p & \frac{1}{2p}+1\end{array}\right)^{\prime}$
\end_inset

.
 Let us consider the market for good 1.
 By the market clearing condition, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\frac{1}{2}+p & = & 2\\
p & = & \frac{3}{2}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Even though a competitive equilibrium price vector exists, the allocation
 is not feasible since 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{11}+x_{12}<1$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, no competitive equilibrium exist in this case.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\emph on
\bar under
Case:
\emph default
\bar default
 
\begin_inset Formula $p=2$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\family roman
\series medium
\shape up
\size normal
\emph off
\bar no
