\begin_layout Standard
Since 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}(x_{i})$
\end_inset

 is differentiable and quasi-concave, then a necessary and sufficient condition
 for the feasible interior points to be Parteo optimal (PO) is that 
\begin_inset Formula $MRS_{x_{11,}x_{12}}=MRS_{x_{21},x_{22}}$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $MRS_{x_{A1,}x_{A2}}=\frac{1}{2}\neq2=MRS_{x_{B1},x_{B2}}$
\end_inset

, then there are no interior PO points.
 However, because indifference curves are linear and agent 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

's indifference curves are steeper, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $MRS_{A}<MRS_{B}$
\end_inset

, then the set of Pareto efficient allocations is given by the upper and
 left edges of the Edgeworth box.
 Thus,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
PO=\{(x_{A},x_{B})\in X_{A}\times X_{B}:x_{A1}x_{B2}=0\ \wedge\ x_{A}+x_{B}=(6,8)\}
\]

\end_inset

is the set of Pareto optimal allocations.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part c.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To calculate the demand functions, we must maximize the utility function
 of each consumer with respect to his budget constraint.
 Since each agent's preferences are monotone, then they are locally non-satiatie
d, and thus, Walras' Law holds.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $p=\frac{p_{2}}{p_{1}}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The constrained utility maximization problem for agent 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 is given by:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\max & u_{A}(x_{A1},x_{A2})=x_{A1}+2x_{A2}\\
\mbox{s.t} & x_{A1}+px_{A2}=4
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Thus, the demand function for agent 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 is:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
x_{A}(p)=\begin{cases}
\left(\begin{array}{c}
0\\
\frac{4}{p}
\end{array}\right) & \mbox{if}\ p<2\\
\left(\begin{array}{c}
x_{A1}\\
2-\frac{x_{A1}}{2}
\end{array}\right),x_{A1}\in\left[0,4\right] & \mbox{if}\ p=2\\
\left(\begin{array}{c}
4\\
0
\end{array}\right) & \mbox{if}\ p>2
\end{cases}
\]

\end_inset

The constrained utility maximization problem for agent 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 is given by:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\max & u_{B}(x_{B1},x_{B2})=2x_{B1}+x_{B2}\\
\mbox{s.t.} & x_{B1}+px_{B2}=2+8p
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Thus, the demand function for agent 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 is:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
x_{B}(p)=\begin{cases}
\left(\begin{array}{c}
0\\
\frac{2}{p}+8
\end{array}\right) & \mbox{if}\ p<\frac{1}{2}\\
\left(\begin{array}{c}
x_{B1}\\
12-2x_{B1}
