\begin_layout Standard
Since 
\begin_inset Formula $n=2$
\end_inset

 and the preference relations are weakly monotonic, then an allocation is
 in the core if and only if it is Pareto optimal (PO) and individually rational
 (IR).
 We have already found the set of PO allocations, so let us now characterize
 the set of IR allocations.
 An allocation 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is individually rational if 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ i:x_{i}\succsim_{i}w_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}(\cdot)$
\end_inset

 represents 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim_{i}$
\end_inset

, then an allocation 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is IR if 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{A1}+2x_{A2}\geq4$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $2x_{B1}+x_{B2}\geq12$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, the set of core allocations is given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
PO\cap IR=\{(x_{A},x_{B})\in X_{A}\times X_{B}:x_{A}+x_{B}=(6,8),x_{A1}x_{B2}=0,x_{A1}+2x_{A2}\geq4,2x_{B1}+x_{B2}\geq12\}
\]

\end_inset


