\begin_layout Standard
Since 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}(x_{i}),i=1,2$
\end_inset

 are differentiable and quasi-concave, then a necessary and sufficient condition
 for the feasible interior points to be Parteo optimal (PO) is that 
\begin_inset Formula $MRS_{x_{11,}x_{12}}=MRS_{x_{21},x_{22}}$
\end_inset

.
 Note, 
\begin_inset Formula $MRS_{1}=\frac{y_{1}}{x_{1}}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $MRS_{2}=\frac{y_{2}}{\alpha x_{2}}$
\end_inset

.
 Setting these two equations equal to one another we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\frac{y_{1}}{x_{1}} & = & \frac{y_{2}}{\alpha x_{2}}\\
\alpha y_{1}(3-x_{1}) & = & x_{1}(3-y_{1})\\
3\alpha y_{1}-\alpha x_{1}y_{1}+x_{1}y_{1} & = & 3x_{1}\\
y_{1}=f(x_{1},\alpha) & = & \frac{3x_{1}}{(x_{1}-\alpha x_{1}+3\alpha)}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\series bold
\bar under
Part b.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
When 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha=2$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{1}=f(x_{1},2)=\frac{3x_{1}}{(6-x_{1})}
\]

\end_inset


\series bold
\bar under
Part c.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To see if 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1}\geq y_{1}$
\end_inset

 at all points 
\begin_inset Formula $(x_{1},y_{1}),(x_{2},y_{2})$
\end_inset

 in the core, we consider
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
0 & \geq & x_{1}(x_{1}-3)\ \mbox{for}\ x_{1}\in(0,3)\\
0 & \geq & x_{1}^{2}-3x_{1}\\
6x_{1}-x_{1}^{2} & \geq & 3x_{1}\\
x_{1} & \geq & \frac{3x_{1}}{(6-x_{1})}=y_{1}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1}\geq y_{1}$
\end_inset

 for all PO allocations, i.e., the entire set of Pareto optimal allocations
 is below the line 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{1}=x_{1}$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $n=2$
\end_inset

 and the preference relations are weakly monotonic, then an allocation is
 in the core if and only if it is Pareto optimal (PO) and individually rational
 (IR).
 So, the set of core allocations is a subset of the set of Pareto optimal
 allocations, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $Core=PO\cap IR\subseteq PO$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, at all points in the core, 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1}\geq y_{1}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

