\begin_layout Standard
Now, suppose the economy is replicated three times.
 In this case, there are three of each type of agent for a total of six
 agents.
 Recall that an allocation is said to be fair if it is both equitable and
 Pareto optimal.
 Important to note, every replication of an envy free allocation must be
 envy free.
 Similarly, every replication of a Pareto optimal allocation must be Pareto
 optimal.
 Accordingly, by our previous result, we know that the following is a fair
 allocation in the replicated economy:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
x_{1}^{A}=x_{1}^{B}=x_{1}^{C}=3+\log(2)\\
y_{1}^{A}=y_{1}^{B}=y_{1}^{C}=\dfrac{4}{3}\\
x_{2}^{A}=x_{2}^{B}=x_{2}^{C}=3-\log(2)\\
y_{2}^{A}=y_{2}^{B}=y_{2}^{C}=\dfrac{8}{3}
\end{gather*}

