\end_inset

.
 Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $\nexists\ d\in X:d\ \mbox{dominates}\ R(p)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof

\emph on
\bar under
Case iii:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset Formula $x\succ y\succ z\succ x,R(P)=x$
\end_inset

.
 There must be at least one agent where 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is his most preferred alternative.
 Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $\nexists\ d\in X:d\ \mbox{dominates}\ R(P)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Hence, 
\begin_inset Formula $\nexists\ d\in X:d\ \mbox{dominates}\ R(P)=x$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

 satisfies the Pareto principle.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part c.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $C(P_{i})$
\end_inset

 denote the choice set of agent 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

.
 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

 is dictatorial if 
\begin_inset Formula $\exists\ i\in N,\forall\ P\in\mathcal{P}:R(P)=C(P_{i})\in X$
\end_inset

.
 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

 is nondictatorial if 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ i\in N,\exists\ P\in\mathcal{P}:R(P)\neq C(P_{i})$
\end_inset

.
 We want to show that 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

 is nondictatorial.
 Take 
\begin_inset Formula $i\in N=\{1,2,3\}$
\end_inset

.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $P$
\end_inset

 be defined as the two remaining agents having the same preferences, and
 these preferences are opposite of agent 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

's.
 Now, majority voting will result in a transitive social ordering with a
 unique best alternative.
 Accorind to 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

, this alternative will be the social choice.
 However, this alternative would not have been chosen by agent 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 since it is his least preferred option.
 Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $R(P)\neq C(P_{i})$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

 is nondictatorial.
\end_layout

